//? Title: GoogLeNet: Parallel Receptive Fields and Auxiliary Supervision
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Combining fine local evidence with broader image context can make a convolutional network
expensive. GoogLeNet evaluates parallel filter scales while reducing channel widths before
large kernels. We inspect all four branches, verify its full topology, and train a compact
nine-module tensor network with explicit main and auxiliary losses.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.2_classic_architectures/googlenet.cpp`.

> The method

Each Inception-v1 module concatenates four SAME-resolution ReLU branches: direct 1×1;
1×1 reduction followed by 3×3; 1×1 reduction followed by 5×5; and 3×3 stride-one
max-pooling followed by a 1×1 projection. Branch order is part of the API. If the six widths
are (a,r3,b,r5,c,d), output channels equal a+b+c+d and kernel MACs per spatial location are

$$
C_{in}(a+r_3+r_5+d)+9r_3b+25r_5c.
$$
\alttext(Only reduced channel widths feed the expensive three-by-three and five-by-five kernels.)

For Table 1 module 3a, widths (64,96,128,16,32,32) at 192 input channels cost 163328
kernel MACs per location. Applying the two large kernels directly to all 192 channels would
cost 393216. This comparison excludes pooling comparisons, biases, activations and memory
traffic; it does not claim the bottlenecks are mathematically interchangeable with every
direct kernel. Multiplying by NHW gives each module's convolution work.

A 7×7 stride-two stem and two smaller convolutions feed nine modules 3a/3b/4a/4b/4c/4d/4e/5a/5b.
Stride-two pools follow 3b and 4e. A spatial mean, 40% dropout and a linear classifier give
main logits. At 224 pixels, the last map is 7×7×1024 and the main model has 6998552 parameters.
Two auxiliary heads follow 4a/4d: VALID 5×5 stride-three average pooling, 1×1 projection,
a 1024-unit ReLU hidden layer, 70% dropout and class logits. Together they add 6379728 scalars.
The objective is

$$
L=CE(z,y)+0.3CE(a_1,y)+0.3CE(a_2,y)+\frac{\lambda}{2}\sum_{W}\|W\|_F^2.
$$
\alttext(The optimizer fits main cross entropy plus two auxiliary cross entropies weighted by point three, plus a kernel penalty.)

Auxiliary gradients reach shared early layers during training; those heads never feed the main
prediction. The report tests this independence by changing auxiliary parameters. LRN uses the
BVLC constants b=1, beta=.75 and summed-square alpha=.0001/5. SAME stride-two padding and
He initialization are explicit adaptations; this is not a bitwise Caffe reproduction.

The CIFAR-10 experiment uses 96-pixel bilinear resizing, quarter widths and 128-unit auxiliary
hidden layers, keeping every module, both heads and the two dropout rates. All training steps
are visible below: 128 fitting, 64 validation and 64 official-test rows, batch eight, 20 epochs,
Adam(.001), kernel penalty lambda=.0001, and padded crop/flip augmentation. Main validation
NLL selects the checkpoint. These bounded runs require cached data and are not ImageNet benchmarks.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/googlenet_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/googlenet_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/googlenet_concept.tikz > build/googlenet_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/googlenet_concept.svg build/googlenet_concept.dvi > build/googlenet_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
