128 fit / 64 validation / 64 official-test rows; 398458 learned scalars; 320 Adam updates.
Best validation epoch 19; NLL 2.180210.
Selected inference training NLL 1.900604; test NLL 2.111705; test accuracy 0.171875.
\image(figures/googlenet_training.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Primary cross entropy from the visible Adam loop. Training uses augmentation and dropout; validation is deterministic. Both use fixed, small subsets.)
\alttext(Primary cross entropy from the visible Adam loop. Training uses augmentation and dropout; validation is deterministic. Both use fixed, small subsets.)


The selected checkpoint is from epoch 19. Its inference training NLL is 1.900604,
validation NLL 2.180210 and official-test-subset NLL 2.111705, with 17.1875% accuracy.
The falling training trace and variable validation trace show why the checkpoint is selected
on held-out NLL. Sixty-four test images do not support a comparison of architectures.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/googlenet_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

Channel reductions control the cost of parallel receptive scales. Auxiliary classifiers add
training signals without adding inputs to the main prediction. The branch arithmetic, full
shape/count checks, gradient and independence checks, and bounded CIFAR-10 run provide separate
evidence for those claims; the short dataset fit is a training demonstration.

> References

- [Szegedy et al. (2015), Going Deeper with Convolutions](https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.4842).
- [BVLC GoogLeNet deployment and normalisation](https://github.com/BVLC/caffe/blob/master/models/bvlc_googlenet/deploy.prototxt).
- [CIFAR-10 dataset and binary-file layout](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html).
